}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" 13194139535278 true] [17592186341241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341242 13194139535278 true] [17592186341241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341243 13194139535278 true] [17592186341241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341244 13194139535278 true] [17592186341241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341245 13194139535278 true] [17592186341241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341246 13194139535278 true] [17592186341241 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535278 true] [17592186341241 1041 "selection" 13194139535278 true] [17592186341241 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341245 13194139535278 true] [17592186341241 1110 #uuid "b10b49c9-e8d8-53bb-a2c7-42b5e6aa0fca" 13194139535278 true] [17592186341242 1034 "-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" 13194139535278 true] [17592186341242 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13